# Next.js Data Fetching Rules

## Server Components Data Fetching
- Use async/await in Server Components
- Fetch data directly in component
- No useEffect needed in Server Components

## Example Server Component Fetching
```tsx
// app/products/page.tsx
async function getProducts() {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/products', {
    cache: 'no-store', // or 'force-cache', 'revalidate'
  });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('Failed to fetch products');
  return res.json();
}

export default async function ProductsPage() {
  const products = await getProducts();
  
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Products</h1>
      <ProductList products={products} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Caching Strategies
- `cache: 'force-cache'` - Default cache, no revalidation
- `cache: 'no-store'` - No cache, always fetch new
- `next: { revalidate: 3600 }` - Revalidate after 3600 seconds

## Example With Revalidation
```tsx
async function getProducts() {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/products', {
    next: { revalidate: 3600 }, // Revalidate every hour
  });
  return res.json();
}
```

## Client-Side Data Fetching
- Use in Client Components
- Use `useEffect` or libraries like SWR, React Query
- Don't fetch in render, only in useEffect or event handlers

## Example Client-Side Fetching
```tsx
'use client';

import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export default function ClientProducts() {
  const [products, setProducts] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('/api/products')
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(data => {
        setProducts(data);
        setLoading(false);
      });
  }, []);

  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;
  
  return <ProductList products={products} />;
}
```

## Server Actions (Next.js 13+)
- Use `'use server'` directive
- Place in separate file or top-level of component

## Example Server Action
```typescript
// app/actions.ts
'use server';

export async function createProduct(formData: FormData) {
  const name = formData.get('name');
  const product = await saveProduct({ name });
  return product;
}
```

```tsx
// app/products/page.tsx
import { createProduct } from './actions';

export default function ProductsPage() {
  async function handleSubmit(formData: FormData) {
    await createProduct(formData);
  }

  return (
    <form action={handleSubmit}>
      <input name="name" />
      <button type="submit">Create</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

## Error Handling
- Always handle errors when fetching data
- Use error.tsx for error boundaries
- Display loading states

## Example Error Handling
```tsx
// app/products/page.tsx
export default async function ProductsPage() {
  try {
    const products = await getProducts();
    return <ProductList products={products} />;
  } catch (error) {
    return <div>Error loading products</div>;
  }
}
```
